#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f7af1dba-6300-56ab-93d2-0f3d1b893dad" :question/math-academy-id "e-21809" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $x\\in[0,6),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/de/de535d3df741309e51bab7305aedcb27507b078771f0931d4914e16c88220926.svg)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "1ff70a32-336a-5ff2-bfe8-0df06eec1a7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21810" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/e2/e25e66141569f5324759eb15a1dcf8b143f5018cf35e957f7a212ba3b45b3556.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\nb_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{8}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi x \\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\nb_n = -\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n}=\\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\cdot \\sin(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bfdb40-b020-5871-af7f-669ab81b634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539484} {:answer/id #uuid "0d477725-039a-569b-974c-cc8168427bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539506} {:answer/id #uuid "133cc9fe-58b4-5563-9de2-f20044a1ad82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539516} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7a4ec2-f370-5681-81e3-c63a1d8a2f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539719} {:answer/id #uuid "a721105a-d718-5aac-86a9-455b5f022623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03bfdb40-b020-5871-af7f-669ab81b634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539484} :answer-field/id #uuid "163adf1f-a5ba-53ac-95f1-d113394667f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "487df78e-b6e4-5124-9586-cb2ed967b312" :question/math-academy-id "q-338784" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0288041e-13a5-5541-bbd8-c13988faf24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427748} {:answer/id #uuid "319ba16a-7d74-5bcd-99b2-bc9850751276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-(-1)^{n})}{4\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427749} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9fb755-7be5-5ae8-be1c-cea88ce12977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427750} {:answer/id #uuid "82973565-ede8-565e-a9ef-215aaa0446b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427751} {:answer/id #uuid "d703d305-1fef-58e0-ac9f-aedced6da080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d703d305-1fef-58e0-ac9f-aedced6da080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427752} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a95669-d006-58f5-a342-6964952c273c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a38bbe2-8cc7-5da0-8136-af259e1c0160" :question/math-academy-id "q-340708" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3e4fe3-02d7-5d29-8ad7-3668f238c80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312201} {:answer/id #uuid "5e546d57-7d43-5fdd-9926-cb08a53da797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|2{x}^{3}-3x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312729} {:answer/id #uuid "72646c31-66f9-59e9-b13a-7b244f98ace6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312874} {:answer/id #uuid "82066e55-50d4-57e0-ac8c-f0b4180a166e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313022} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7964f5-e30a-59a4-bfc5-74cffaad8946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2|x{|}^{3}-3|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e3e4fe3-02d7-5d29-8ad7-3668f238c80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312201} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf43efd4-4be2-5e7b-a5ea-b1f07093d073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e12be66-5682-57ef-8004-80dc62d4ed67" :question/math-academy-id "q-340871" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-2{x}^{3}+3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ba050f-0e34-5d8c-83aa-f3242fc48a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379577} {:answer/id #uuid "995e1ab4-b124-5ed9-856a-c8dd7f49f2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-(-1)^{n})}{6\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379578} {:answer/id #uuid "b876efa2-19b0-53d0-8e5f-5f77d454f201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379579} {:answer/id #uuid "2641e9c0-dea9-5f20-8423-8964d19b533f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379580} {:answer/id #uuid "7a69e266-76db-54f7-8f88-ce76760b76fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a69e266-76db-54f7-8f88-ce76760b76fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379581} :answer-field/id #uuid "acddc9da-f416-502b-a8b1-7e3b25bc89e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0702963-3f76-5783-ba13-4c9e296283ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-340895" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fd64ac7-7e08-59ab-bbc4-4251ea963cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{((-1)^{n}-1)}{8\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379584} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3ac050-5e12-51cf-8cda-18b9be41235f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379585} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5a389f-8a44-5527-a66c-161fc6d48510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379586} {:answer/id #uuid "cba62257-ac04-5987-b049-6fc916e0aceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379587} {:answer/id #uuid "837645a9-f89d-5d26-adaf-7ea72da87792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba62257-ac04-5987-b049-6fc916e0aceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379587} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed63a95-9aef-53fb-825c-118fcd9dacc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3d82ee9-9c73-5ee8-96d1-0448b9926883" :question/math-academy-id "q-340906" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc2695f-0e15-5691-a028-b150d466dcf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312316} {:answer/id #uuid "725ea555-7d4c-5eaf-95eb-128a98f4ed38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312872} {:answer/id #uuid "f347fe48-c18b-5b04-9912-af36b33ea06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313921} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8ec435-d3fb-5f32-883e-f63f94659176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725ea555-7d4c-5eaf-95eb-128a98f4ed38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312872} :answer-field/id #uuid "da0d16eb-266b-580b-b823-2ca3bad44068" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f25da9e-c33a-5999-a8c4-ac447897722c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312485} {:answer/id #uuid "525fe023-9b2c-5613-ac7a-b31e065a357b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312643} {:answer/id #uuid "b69786fc-4a61-5bdb-88d2-2cf63ec7fbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313393} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe655ac-4aeb-5468-80b5-b24c11370919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f25da9e-c33a-5999-a8c4-ac447897722c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312485} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece722c4-3730-5a44-bfa8-824b101a061a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f36f74d-9ded-58b3-8942-de350905b5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340922" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbbdbe28-1ec6-5a1d-af09-19d933939c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(1-(-1)^{n+1})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379591} {:answer/id #uuid "302c1b7d-6236-5a16-86ec-4812b9d509c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379592} {:answer/id #uuid "c04ff30f-41a8-5ef8-bcda-b30a31efc2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379593} {:answer/id #uuid "472c1e86-798e-5dc4-b7c3-448c0ac84c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04ff30f-41a8-5ef8-bcda-b30a31efc2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379593} :answer-field/id #uuid "79a6378f-34e6-5fc5-aa94-a01fd4ad1963" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4e611af-caa9-51f2-a7d5-41c09f3cb745" :question/math-academy-id "q-340927" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=6x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}{[\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]}_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }(-\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n}=\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669c79c2-1922-5d4f-8294-a85dd5e7d7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669c79c2-1922-5d4f-8294-a85dd5e7d7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379597} :answer-field/id #uuid "62443fee-fe96-5815-bb89-8967be450b9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0188791e-a7fa-5d1c-b905-419c47766caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-341203" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in [0,12),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-12,12).$ What is the value of $f(-4)?$\n\n$f(-4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,12),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,12), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-12,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-12,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}-6(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-(-4)^{2}-6(-4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55fed7ab-a91c-50d6-aa4f-e191cbf584c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55fed7ab-a91c-50d6-aa4f-e191cbf584c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312667} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bbefba-2fd6-5ec2-b297-2bf9ad8d5f88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9a83be1-0157-5d64-aa36-c77e159ee194" :question/math-academy-id "q-341210" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-{x}^{2}+5x$ for $x\\in [0,8),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-8,8).$ What is the value of $f(-3)?$\n\n$f(-3)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,8),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,8), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-8,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-8,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-(-x)^{2}+5(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+5(-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
